---
title: Custom Features (Solid) Guide
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use getters for reactive inputs such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" when passing Solid signals to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTable"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this guide, we'll cover how to extend TanStack Table with custom features, and along the way, we'll learn more about how the TanStack Table v9 codebase is structured and how it works."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tanstack-table-strives-to-be-lean","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table Strives to be Lean"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table has a core set of features that are built into the library such as sorting, filtering, pagination, etc. We've received a lot of requests and sometimes even some well thought out PRs to add even more features to the library. While we are always open to improving the library, we also want to make sure that TanStack Table remains a lean library that does not include too much bloat and code that is unlikely to be used in most use cases. Not every PR can, or should, be accepted into the core library, even if it does solve a real problem. This can be frustrating to developers when TanStack Table solves 90% of their use case, but they need a little bit more control."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table has always been built in a way that allows it to be highly extensible (at least since v7). The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table"},{"type":"text","value":" instance that is returned from whichever framework adapter that you are using ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTable"},{"type":"text","value":" for Solid, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" for React, etc) is a plain JavaScript object that can have extra properties or APIs added to it. It has always been possible to use composition to add custom logic, state, and APIs to the table instance. Libraries like "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/KevinVandy/material-react-table/blob/v2/packages/material-react-table/src/hooks/useMRT_TableInstance.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Material React Table"}]},{"type":"text","value":" have simply created custom wrappers around their adapter's table function to extend the table instance with custom functionality."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In v9, TanStack Table uses the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" option (via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":") to declare which features your table uses. This enables tree-shaking: you only bundle the code for the features you need. You can add custom features to the table instance in exactly the same way as the built-in features."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In v9, features are opt-in. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({ ... })"},{"type":"text","value":" to declare which features your table uses, including custom features."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-tanstack-table-features-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How TanStack Table Features Work"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table's source code is arguably somewhat simple (at least we think so). All code for each feature is split up into its own object/file with instantiation methods to create initial state, default table and column options, and API methods that can be added to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":" instances."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All of the functionality of a feature object can be described with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" type that is exported from TanStack Table. This type is a TypeScript interface that describes the shape of a feature object needed to create a feature."}]},{"type":"code","value":"export interface TableFeature {\n  assignCellPrototype?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  assignColumnPrototype?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  assignHeaderPrototype?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  assignRowPrototype?: <TFeatures extends TableFeatures, TData extends RowData>(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  constructTableAPIs?: <TFeatures extends TableFeatures, TData extends RowData>(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  initTableInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  getDefaultColumnDef?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >() => ColumnDefBase_All<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\n  getDefaultTableOptions?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => Partial<TableOptions_All<TFeatures, TData>>\n  getInitialState?: (initialState: Partial<TableState_All>) => TableState_All\n  initCellInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >(\n    cell: Cell<TFeatures, TData, TValue>,\n  ) => void\n  initColumnInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >(\n    column: Column<TFeatures, TData, TValue>,\n  ) => void\n  initHeaderGroupInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    headerGroup: HeaderGroup<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  initHeaderInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >(\n    header: Header<TFeatures, TData, TValue>,\n  ) => void\n  initRowInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    row: Row<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  resetTableInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This might be a bit confusing, so let's break down what each of these methods does:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"default-options-and-initial-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default Options and Initial State"}]},{"type":"html","value":"<br />"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"getdefaulttableoptions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"getDefaultTableOptions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getDefaultTableOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" method in a table feature is responsible for setting the default table options for that feature. For example, in the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/column-resizing/columnResizingFeature.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resizing"}]},{"type":"text","value":" feature, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getDefaultTableOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" method sets the default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizeMode"},{"type":"text","value":" option with a default value of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"onEnd\""},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"html","value":"<br />"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"getdefaultcolumndef","children":[{"type":"text","value":"getDefaultColumnDef"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getDefaultColumnDef"},{"type":"text","value":" method in a table feature is responsible for setting the default column options for that feature. For example, in the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-sorting/rowSortingFeature.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting"}]},{"type":"text","value":" feature, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getDefaultColumnDef"},{"type":"text","value":" method sets the default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortUndefined"},{"type":"text","value":" column option with a default value of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"html","value":"<br />"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"getinitialstate","children":[{"type":"text","value":"getInitialState"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getInitialState"},{"type":"text","value":" method in a table feature is responsible for setting the default state for that feature. For example, in the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-pagination/rowPaginationFeature.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination"}]},{"type":"text","value":" feature, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getInitialState"},{"type":"text","value":" method sets the default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageSize"},{"type":"text","value":" state with a value of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"10"},{"type":"text","value":" and the default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" state with a value of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"api-creators","children":[{"type":"text","value":"API Creators"}]},{"type":"html","value":"<br />"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"inittableinstancedata-and-resettableinstancedata","children":[{"type":"text","value":"initTableInstanceData and resetTableInstanceData"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initTableInstanceData"},{"type":"text","value":" for mutable, non-reactive data that belongs to one table instance, such as an interaction anchor or an imperative cache. It runs once after table options, state atoms, and the store have been created. Features are processed in a single pass in registration order; each feature's initialization hook runs just before that feature's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"constructTableAPIs"},{"type":"text","value":" hook, so hooks may rely on data and APIs from features registered earlier."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resetTableInstanceData"},{"type":"text","value":" to clear that transient data when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" runs. Reset hooks run after internally owned table state atoms have been restored to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.initialState"},{"type":"text","value":". They do not reset table state slices or externally controlled state, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" does not rerun "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initTableInstanceData"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep API assignment in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"constructTableAPIs"},{"type":"text","value":"; initialization and reset hooks are for data owned by the feature."}]},{"type":"html","value":"<br />"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"constructtableapis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"constructTableAPIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"constructTableAPIs"},{"type":"text","value":" method in a table feature is exclusively responsible for adding methods to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table"},{"type":"text","value":" instance. It runs after all feature-owned table instance data has been initialized. For example, in the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-selection/rowSelectionFeature.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Selection"}]},{"type":"text","value":" feature, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"constructTableAPIs"},{"type":"text","value":" method adds many table instance API methods such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toggleAllRowsSelected"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getIsAllRowsSelected"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getIsSomeRowsSelected"},{"type":"text","value":", etc. So then, when you call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.toggleAllRowsSelected()"},{"type":"text","value":", you are calling a method that was added to the table instance by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSelectionFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" feature."}]},{"type":"html","value":"<br />"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"assignheaderprototype-and-initheaderinstancedata","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assignHeaderPrototype and initHeaderInstanceData"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"assignHeaderPrototype"},{"type":"text","value":" method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":" prototype. For example, the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/column-sizing/columnSizingFeature.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Sizing"}]},{"type":"text","value":" feature adds header instance API methods such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getStart"},{"type":"text","value":". So then, when you call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header.getStart()"},{"type":"text","value":", you are calling a method that was added by the column sizing feature. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initHeaderInstanceData"},{"type":"text","value":" method is available for per-header instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. It runs during header construction, before sub-headers are populated and before the header is linked to its header group. Headers are reconstructed whenever header groups recompute, so it reruns on every rebuild."}]},{"type":"html","value":"<br />"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"initheadergroupinstancedata","children":[{"type":"text","value":"initHeaderGroupInstanceData"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initHeaderGroupInstanceData"},{"type":"text","value":" method is available for per-header-group instance data. Header groups have no shared prototype, so this is their only per-instance extension point. It runs after a header group's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"depth"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", and fully populated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headers"},{"type":"text","value":" array have been assigned, and reruns whenever header groups are rebuilt."}]},{"type":"html","value":"<br />"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"assigncolumnprototype-and-initcolumninstancedata","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assignColumnPrototype and initColumnInstanceData"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"assignColumnPrototype"},{"type":"text","value":" method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column"},{"type":"text","value":" prototype. For example, the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-sorting/rowSortingFeature.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting"}]},{"type":"text","value":" feature adds column instance API methods such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getNextSortingOrder"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toggleSorting"},{"type":"text","value":", etc. So then, when you call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.toggleSorting()"},{"type":"text","value":", you are calling a method that was added by the row sorting feature. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initColumnInstanceData"},{"type":"text","value":" method is available for per-column instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. For example, the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-aggregation/rowAggregationFeature.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aggregation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" feature uses it to set up a per-column aggregation cache."}]},{"type":"html","value":"<br />"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"assignrowprototype-and-initrowinstancedata","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assignRowPrototype and initRowInstanceData"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"assignRowPrototype"},{"type":"text","value":" method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row"},{"type":"text","value":" prototype. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initRowInstanceData"},{"type":"text","value":" method is available for per-row instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. For example, the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-selection/rowSelectionFeature.ts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Selection"}]},{"type":"text","value":" feature adds row instance API methods such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toggleSelected"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getIsSelected"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"html","value":"<br />"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"assigncellprototype-and-initcellinstancedata","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assignCellPrototype and initCellInstanceData"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"assignCellPrototype"},{"type":"text","value":" method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":" prototype. For example, Column Grouping adds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getIsGrouped"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getIsPlaceholder"},{"type":"text","value":", while Aggregation adds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getIsAggregated"},{"type":"text","value":". The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initCellInstanceData"},{"type":"text","value":" method is available for per-cell instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. Cells are constructed lazily on first access per row/column pair and cached, so it runs once per cell instance."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"adding-a-custom-feature","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adding a Custom Feature"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let's walk through making a custom table feature for a hypothetical use case. Let's say we want to add a feature to the table instance that allows the user to change the \"density\" (padding of cells) of the table."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is no Solid custom-plugin example in the repository yet (the example currently exists for React, Preact, and Angular), but the feature object itself is framework-agnostic, so the steps below translate directly. Here's an in-depth look at the steps to create a custom feature."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"step-1-set-up-typescript-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 1: Set up TypeScript Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assuming you want the same full type-safety that the built-in features in TanStack Table have, let's set up all of the TypeScript types for our new feature. We'll create types for new table options, state, and table instance API methods."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These types are following the naming convention used internally within TanStack Table, but you can name them whatever you want. We are not adding these types to TanStack Table yet, but we'll do that in the next step."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// define types for our new feature's custom state\nexport type DensityState = 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg'\nexport interface TableState_Density {\n  density: DensityState\n}\n\n// define types for our new feature's table options\nexport interface TableOptions_Density {\n  enableDensity?: boolean\n  onDensityChange?: OnChangeFn<DensityState>\n}\n\n// Define types for our new feature's table APIs\nexport interface Table_Density {\n  setDensity: (updater: Updater<DensityState>) => void\n  toggleDensity: (value?: DensityState) => void\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"step-2-add-the-feature-to-tanstack-table-s-feature-maps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 2: Add the Feature to TanStack Table's Feature Maps"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table uses the keys passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({ ... })"},{"type":"text","value":" to infer which feature state, options, and APIs exist on a table. To make a custom feature key type-safe, add it to the exported "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Plugins"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableState_FeatureMap"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableOptions_FeatureMap"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Table_FeatureMap"},{"type":"text","value":" interfaces with declaration merging."}]},{"type":"code","value":"declare module '@tanstack/solid-table' {\n  interface Plugins {\n    densityPlugin: TableFeature\n  }\n\n  interface TableState_FeatureMap {\n    densityPlugin: TableState_Density\n  }\n\n  interface TableOptions_FeatureMap<\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  > {\n    densityPlugin: TableOptions_Density\n  }\n\n  interface Table_FeatureMap<\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  > {\n    densityPlugin: Table_Density\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once the feature is registered this way, TypeScript can infer the feature's state, options, and APIs only on tables whose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"densityPlugin"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"step-3-create-the-feature-object","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 3: Create the Feature Object"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With all of that TypeScript setup out of the way, we can now create the feature object for our new feature. This is where we define all of the methods that will be added to the table instance."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" type to ensure that you are creating the feature object correctly. If the TypeScript types are set up correctly, you should have no TypeScript errors when you create the feature object with the new state, options, and instance APIs."}]},{"type":"code","value":"export const densityPlugin: TableFeature = {\n  // define the new feature's initial state\n  getInitialState: (initialState) => {\n    return {\n      density: 'md',\n      ...initialState, // must come last\n    }\n  },\n\n  // define the new feature's default options\n  getDefaultTableOptions: (table) => {\n    return {\n      enableDensity: true,\n      onDensityChange: makeStateUpdater('density', table),\n    }\n  },\n  // if you need to add a default column definition...\n  // getDefaultColumnDef: () => {},\n\n  // define the new feature's table instance methods\n  constructTableAPIs: (table) => {\n    assignTableAPIs('densityPlugin', table, {\n      table_setDensity: {\n        fn: (updater: Updater<DensityState>) => {\n          const safeUpdater: Updater<DensityState> = (old) => {\n            const newState = functionalUpdate(updater, old)\n            return newState\n          }\n          return table.options.onDensityChange?.(safeUpdater)\n        },\n      },\n      table_toggleDensity: {\n        fn: (value?: DensityState) => {\n          const safeUpdater: Updater<DensityState> = (old) => {\n            if (value) return value\n            return old === 'lg' ? 'md' : old === 'md' ? 'sm' : 'lg'\n          }\n          return table.options.onDensityChange?.(safeUpdater)\n        },\n      },\n    })\n  },\n\n  // if you need to add row instance APIs...\n  // assignRowPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initRowInstanceData: (row) => {},\n  // if you need to add cell instance APIs...\n  // assignCellPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initCellInstanceData: (cell) => {},\n  // if you need to add column instance APIs...\n  // assignColumnPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initColumnInstanceData: (column) => {},\n  // if you need to add header instance APIs...\n  // assignHeaderPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initHeaderInstanceData: (header) => {},\n  // if you need to add header group instance data...\n  // initHeaderGroupInstanceData: (headerGroup) => {},\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"step-4-add-the-feature-to-the-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 4: Add the Feature to the Table"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now that we have our feature object, we can add it to the table instance by including it in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":" call and passing the result to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" option when we create the table instance."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({ densityPlugin })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //..\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"step-5-use-the-feature-in-your-application","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 5: Use the Feature in Your Application"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now that the feature is added to the table instance, you can use the new instance APIs, options, and state in your application. Here the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"density"},{"type":"text","value":" state is controlled with a Solid signal and the new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDensityChange"},{"type":"text","value":" option:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({ densityPlugin })\n\nconst [density, setDensity] = createSignal<DensityState>('md')\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    get density() {\n      return density() // passing the density state to the table, TS is still happy :)\n    },\n  },\n  onDensityChange: setDensity,\n})\n//...\nreturn (\n  <td\n    style={{\n      //using our new feature in the code\n      padding: density() === 'sm' ? '4px' : density() === 'md' ? '8px' : '16px',\n      transition: 'padding 0.2s',\n    }}\n  >\n    <table.FlexRender cell={cell} />\n  </td>\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"do-we-have-to-do-it-this-way","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do We Have to Do It This Way?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is just a new way to integrate custom code alongside the built-in features in TanStack Table. In our example up above, we could have just as easily stored the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"density"},{"type":"text","value":" state in a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSignal"},{"type":"text","value":", defined our own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toggleDensity"},{"type":"text","value":" handler wherever, and just used it in our code separately from the table instance. Building table features alongside TanStack Table instead of deeply integrating them into the table instance is still a perfectly valid way to build custom features. Depending on your use case, this may or may not be the cleanest way to extend TanStack Table with custom features."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"tanstack-table-strives-to-be-lean","text":"TanStack Table Strives to be Lean","level":2},{"id":"how-tanstack-table-features-work","text":"How TanStack Table Features Work","level":2},{"id":"default-options-and-initial-state","text":"Default Options and Initial State","level":3},{"id":"getdefaulttableoptions","text":"getDefaultTableOptions","level":4},{"id":"getdefaultcolumndef","text":"getDefaultColumnDef","level":4},{"id":"getinitialstate","text":"getInitialState","level":4},{"id":"api-creators","text":"API Creators","level":3},{"id":"inittableinstancedata-and-resettableinstancedata","text":"initTableInstanceData and resetTableInstanceData","level":4},{"id":"constructtableapis","text":"constructTableAPIs","level":4},{"id":"assignheaderprototype-and-initheaderinstancedata","text":"assignHeaderPrototype and initHeaderInstanceData","level":4},{"id":"initheadergroupinstancedata","text":"initHeaderGroupInstanceData","level":4},{"id":"assigncolumnprototype-and-initcolumninstancedata","text":"assignColumnPrototype and initColumnInstanceData","level":4},{"id":"assignrowprototype-and-initrowinstancedata","text":"assignRowPrototype and initRowInstanceData","level":4},{"id":"assigncellprototype-and-initcellinstancedata","text":"assignCellPrototype and initCellInstanceData","level":4},{"id":"adding-a-custom-feature","text":"Adding a Custom Feature","level":2},{"id":"step-1-set-up-typescript-types","text":"Step 1: Set up TypeScript Types","level":3},{"id":"step-2-add-the-feature-to-tanstack-table-s-feature-maps","text":"Step 2: Add the Feature to TanStack Table's Feature Maps","level":3},{"id":"step-3-create-the-feature-object","text":"Step 3: Create the Feature Object","level":3},{"id":"step-4-add-the-feature-to-the-table","text":"Step 4: Add the Feature to the Table","level":3},{"id":"step-5-use-the-feature-in-your-application","text":"Step 5: Use the Feature in Your Application","level":3},{"id":"do-we-have-to-do-it-this-way","text":"Do We Have to Do It This Way?","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nUse getters for reactive inputs such as `data` when passing Solid signals to `createTable`.\n\nIn this guide, we'll cover how to extend TanStack Table with custom features, and along the way, we'll learn more about how the TanStack Table v9 codebase is structured and how it works.\n\n## TanStack Table Strives to be Lean\n\nTanStack Table has a core set of features that are built into the library such as sorting, filtering, pagination, etc. We've received a lot of requests and sometimes even some well thought out PRs to add even more features to the library. While we are always open to improving the library, we also want to make sure that TanStack Table remains a lean library that does not include too much bloat and code that is unlikely to be used in most use cases. Not every PR can, or should, be accepted into the core library, even if it does solve a real problem. This can be frustrating to developers when TanStack Table solves 90% of their use case, but they need a little bit more control.\n\nTanStack Table has always been built in a way that allows it to be highly extensible (at least since v7). The `table` instance that is returned from whichever framework adapter that you are using (`createTable` for Solid, `useTable` for React, etc) is a plain JavaScript object that can have extra properties or APIs added to it. It has always been possible to use composition to add custom logic, state, and APIs to the table instance. Libraries like [Material React Table](https://github.com/KevinVandy/material-react-table/blob/v2/packages/material-react-table/src/hooks/useMRT_TableInstance.ts) have simply created custom wrappers around their adapter's table function to extend the table instance with custom functionality.\n\nIn v9, TanStack Table uses the `features` option (via `tableFeatures()`) to declare which features your table uses. This enables tree-shaking: you only bundle the code for the features you need. You can add custom features to the table instance in exactly the same way as the built-in features.\n\n> In v9, features are opt-in. Use `tableFeatures({ ... })` to declare which features your table uses, including custom features.\n\n## How TanStack Table Features Work\n\nTanStack Table's source code is arguably somewhat simple (at least we think so). All code for each feature is split up into its own object/file with instantiation methods to create initial state, default table and column options, and API methods that can be added to the `table`, `header`, `column`, `row`, and `cell` instances.\n\nAll of the functionality of a feature object can be described with the `TableFeature` type that is exported from TanStack Table. This type is a TypeScript interface that describes the shape of a feature object needed to create a feature.\n\n```ts\nexport interface TableFeature {\n  assignCellPrototype?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  assignColumnPrototype?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  assignHeaderPrototype?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  assignRowPrototype?: <TFeatures extends TableFeatures, TData extends RowData>(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  constructTableAPIs?: <TFeatures extends TableFeatures, TData extends RowData>(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  initTableInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  getDefaultColumnDef?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >() => ColumnDefBase_All<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\n  getDefaultTableOptions?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => Partial<TableOptions_All<TFeatures, TData>>\n  getInitialState?: (initialState: Partial<TableState_All>) => TableState_All\n  initCellInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >(\n    cell: Cell<TFeatures, TData, TValue>,\n  ) => void\n  initColumnInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >(\n    column: Column<TFeatures, TData, TValue>,\n  ) => void\n  initHeaderGroupInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    headerGroup: HeaderGroup<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  initHeaderInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >(\n    header: Header<TFeatures, TData, TValue>,\n  ) => void\n  initRowInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    row: Row<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  resetTableInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n}\n```\n\nThis might be a bit confusing, so let's break down what each of these methods does:\n\n### Default Options and Initial State\n\n<br />\n\n#### getDefaultTableOptions\n\nThe `getDefaultTableOptions` method in a table feature is responsible for setting the default table options for that feature. For example, in the [Column Resizing](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/column-resizing/columnResizingFeature.ts) feature, the `getDefaultTableOptions` method sets the default `columnResizeMode` option with a default value of `\"onEnd\"`.\n\n<br />\n\n#### getDefaultColumnDef\n\nThe `getDefaultColumnDef` method in a table feature is responsible for setting the default column options for that feature. For example, in the [Sorting](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-sorting/rowSortingFeature.ts) feature, the `getDefaultColumnDef` method sets the default `sortUndefined` column option with a default value of `1`.\n\n<br />\n\n#### getInitialState\n\nThe `getInitialState` method in a table feature is responsible for setting the default state for that feature. For example, in the [Pagination](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-pagination/rowPaginationFeature.ts) feature, the `getInitialState` method sets the default `pageSize` state with a value of `10` and the default `pageIndex` state with a value of `0`.\n\n### API Creators\n\n<br />\n\n#### initTableInstanceData and resetTableInstanceData\n\nUse `initTableInstanceData` for mutable, non-reactive data that belongs to one table instance, such as an interaction anchor or an imperative cache. It runs once after table options, state atoms, and the store have been created. Features are processed in a single pass in registration order; each feature's initialization hook runs just before that feature's `constructTableAPIs` hook, so hooks may rely on data and APIs from features registered earlier.\n\nUse `resetTableInstanceData` to clear that transient data when `table.reset()` runs. Reset hooks run after internally owned table state atoms have been restored to `table.initialState`. They do not reset table state slices or externally controlled state, and `table.reset()` does not rerun `initTableInstanceData`.\n\nKeep API assignment in `constructTableAPIs`; initialization and reset hooks are for data owned by the feature.\n\n<br />\n\n#### constructTableAPIs\n\nThe `constructTableAPIs` method in a table feature is exclusively responsible for adding methods to the `table` instance. It runs after all feature-owned table instance data has been initialized. For example, in the [Row Selection](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-selection/rowSelectionFeature.ts) feature, the `constructTableAPIs` method adds many table instance API methods such as `toggleAllRowsSelected`, `getIsAllRowsSelected`, `getIsSomeRowsSelected`, etc. So then, when you call `table.toggleAllRowsSelected()`, you are calling a method that was added to the table instance by the `rowSelectionFeature` feature.\n\n<br />\n\n#### assignHeaderPrototype and initHeaderInstanceData\n\nThe `assignHeaderPrototype` method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared `header` prototype. For example, the [Column Sizing](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/column-sizing/columnSizingFeature.ts) feature adds header instance API methods such as `getStart`. So then, when you call `header.getStart()`, you are calling a method that was added by the column sizing feature. The `initHeaderInstanceData` method is available for per-header instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. It runs during header construction, before sub-headers are populated and before the header is linked to its header group. Headers are reconstructed whenever header groups recompute, so it reruns on every rebuild.\n\n<br />\n\n#### initHeaderGroupInstanceData\n\nThe `initHeaderGroupInstanceData` method is available for per-header-group instance data. Header groups have no shared prototype, so this is their only per-instance extension point. It runs after a header group's `depth`, `id`, and fully populated `headers` array have been assigned, and reruns whenever header groups are rebuilt.\n\n<br />\n\n#### assignColumnPrototype and initColumnInstanceData\n\nThe `assignColumnPrototype` method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared `column` prototype. For example, the [Sorting](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-sorting/rowSortingFeature.ts) feature adds column instance API methods such as `getNextSortingOrder`, `toggleSorting`, etc. So then, when you call `column.toggleSorting()`, you are calling a method that was added by the row sorting feature. The `initColumnInstanceData` method is available for per-column instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. For example, the [Aggregation](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-aggregation/rowAggregationFeature.ts) feature uses it to set up a per-column aggregation cache.\n\n<br />\n\n#### assignRowPrototype and initRowInstanceData\n\nThe `assignRowPrototype` method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared `row` prototype. The `initRowInstanceData` method is available for per-row instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. For example, the [Row Selection](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-selection/rowSelectionFeature.ts) feature adds row instance API methods such as `toggleSelected` and `getIsSelected`.\n\n<br />\n\n#### assignCellPrototype and initCellInstanceData\n\nThe `assignCellPrototype` method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared `cell` prototype. For example, Column Grouping adds `getIsGrouped` and `getIsPlaceholder`, while Aggregation adds `getIsAggregated`. The `initCellInstanceData` method is available for per-cell instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. Cells are constructed lazily on first access per row/column pair and cached, so it runs once per cell instance.\n\n## Adding a Custom Feature\n\nLet's walk through making a custom table feature for a hypothetical use case. Let's say we want to add a feature to the table instance that allows the user to change the \"density\" (padding of cells) of the table.\n\nThere is no Solid custom-plugin example in the repository yet (the example currently exists for React, Preact, and Angular), but the feature object itself is framework-agnostic, so the steps below translate directly. Here's an in-depth look at the steps to create a custom feature.\n\n### Step 1: Set up TypeScript Types\n\nAssuming you want the same full type-safety that the built-in features in TanStack Table have, let's set up all of the TypeScript types for our new feature. We'll create types for new table options, state, and table instance API methods.\n\nThese types are following the naming convention used internally within TanStack Table, but you can name them whatever you want. We are not adding these types to TanStack Table yet, but we'll do that in the next step.\n\n```ts\n// define types for our new feature's custom state\nexport type DensityState = 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg'\nexport interface TableState_Density {\n  density: DensityState\n}\n\n// define types for our new feature's table options\nexport interface TableOptions_Density {\n  enableDensity?: boolean\n  onDensityChange?: OnChangeFn<DensityState>\n}\n\n// Define types for our new feature's table APIs\nexport interface Table_Density {\n  setDensity: (updater: Updater<DensityState>) => void\n  toggleDensity: (value?: DensityState) => void\n}\n```\n\n### Step 2: Add the Feature to TanStack Table's Feature Maps\n\nTanStack Table uses the keys passed to `tableFeatures({ ... })` to infer which feature state, options, and APIs exist on a table. To make a custom feature key type-safe, add it to the exported `Plugins`, `TableState_FeatureMap`, `TableOptions_FeatureMap`, and `Table_FeatureMap` interfaces with declaration merging.\n\n```ts\ndeclare module '@tanstack/solid-table' {\n  interface Plugins {\n    densityPlugin: TableFeature\n  }\n\n  interface TableState_FeatureMap {\n    densityPlugin: TableState_Density\n  }\n\n  interface TableOptions_FeatureMap<\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  > {\n    densityPlugin: TableOptions_Density\n  }\n\n  interface Table_FeatureMap<\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  > {\n    densityPlugin: Table_Density\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOnce the feature is registered this way, TypeScript can infer the feature's state, options, and APIs only on tables whose `features` include `densityPlugin`.\n\n### Step 3: Create the Feature Object\n\nWith all of that TypeScript setup out of the way, we can now create the feature object for our new feature. This is where we define all of the methods that will be added to the table instance.\n\nUse the `TableFeature` type to ensure that you are creating the feature object correctly. If the TypeScript types are set up correctly, you should have no TypeScript errors when you create the feature object with the new state, options, and instance APIs.\n\n```ts\nexport const densityPlugin: TableFeature = {\n  // define the new feature's initial state\n  getInitialState: (initialState) => {\n    return {\n      density: 'md',\n      ...initialState, // must come last\n    }\n  },\n\n  // define the new feature's default options\n  getDefaultTableOptions: (table) => {\n    return {\n      enableDensity: true,\n      onDensityChange: makeStateUpdater('density', table),\n    }\n  },\n  // if you need to add a default column definition...\n  // getDefaultColumnDef: () => {},\n\n  // define the new feature's table instance methods\n  constructTableAPIs: (table) => {\n    assignTableAPIs('densityPlugin', table, {\n      table_setDensity: {\n        fn: (updater: Updater<DensityState>) => {\n          const safeUpdater: Updater<DensityState> = (old) => {\n            const newState = functionalUpdate(updater, old)\n            return newState\n          }\n          return table.options.onDensityChange?.(safeUpdater)\n        },\n      },\n      table_toggleDensity: {\n        fn: (value?: DensityState) => {\n          const safeUpdater: Updater<DensityState> = (old) => {\n            if (value) return value\n            return old === 'lg' ? 'md' : old === 'md' ? 'sm' : 'lg'\n          }\n          return table.options.onDensityChange?.(safeUpdater)\n        },\n      },\n    })\n  },\n\n  // if you need to add row instance APIs...\n  // assignRowPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initRowInstanceData: (row) => {},\n  // if you need to add cell instance APIs...\n  // assignCellPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initCellInstanceData: (cell) => {},\n  // if you need to add column instance APIs...\n  // assignColumnPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initColumnInstanceData: (column) => {},\n  // if you need to add header instance APIs...\n  // assignHeaderPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initHeaderInstanceData: (header) => {},\n  // if you need to add header group instance data...\n  // initHeaderGroupInstanceData: (headerGroup) => {},\n}\n```\n\n### Step 4: Add the Feature to the Table\n\nNow that we have our feature object, we can add it to the table instance by including it in the `tableFeatures()` call and passing the result to the `features` option when we create the table instance.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({ densityPlugin })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //..\n})\n```\n\n### Step 5: Use the Feature in Your Application\n\nNow that the feature is added to the table instance, you can use the new instance APIs, options, and state in your application. Here the `density` state is controlled with a Solid signal and the new `onDensityChange` option:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({ densityPlugin })\n\nconst [density, setDensity] = createSignal<DensityState>('md')\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    get density() {\n      return density() // passing the density state to the table, TS is still happy :)\n    },\n  },\n  onDensityChange: setDensity,\n})\n//...\nreturn (\n  <td\n    style={{\n      //using our new feature in the code\n      padding: density() === 'sm' ? '4px' : density() === 'md' ? '8px' : '16px',\n      transition: 'padding 0.2s',\n    }}\n  >\n    <table.FlexRender cell={cell} />\n  </td>\n)\n```\n\n### Do We Have to Do It This Way?\n\nThis is just a new way to integrate custom code alongside the built-in features in TanStack Table. In our example up above, we could have just as easily stored the `density` state in a `createSignal`, defined our own `toggleDensity` handler wherever, and just used it in our code separately from the table instance. Building table features alongside TanStack Table instead of deeply integrating them into the table instance is still a perfectly valid way to build custom features. Depending on your use case, this may or may not be the cleanest way to extend TanStack Table with custom features.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Custom Features (Solid) Guide\n---\n\nUse getters for reactive inputs such as `data` when passing Solid signals to `createTable`.\n\nIn this guide, we'll cover how to extend TanStack Table with custom features, and along the way, we'll learn more about how the TanStack Table v9 codebase is structured and how it works.\n\n## TanStack Table Strives to be Lean\n\nTanStack Table has a core set of features that are built into the library such as sorting, filtering, pagination, etc. We've received a lot of requests and sometimes even some well thought out PRs to add even more features to the library. While we are always open to improving the library, we also want to make sure that TanStack Table remains a lean library that does not include too much bloat and code that is unlikely to be used in most use cases. Not every PR can, or should, be accepted into the core library, even if it does solve a real problem. This can be frustrating to developers when TanStack Table solves 90% of their use case, but they need a little bit more control.\n\nTanStack Table has always been built in a way that allows it to be highly extensible (at least since v7). The `table` instance that is returned from whichever framework adapter that you are using (`createTable` for Solid, `useTable` for React, etc) is a plain JavaScript object that can have extra properties or APIs added to it. It has always been possible to use composition to add custom logic, state, and APIs to the table instance. Libraries like [Material React Table](https://github.com/KevinVandy/material-react-table/blob/v2/packages/material-react-table/src/hooks/useMRT_TableInstance.ts) have simply created custom wrappers around their adapter's table function to extend the table instance with custom functionality.\n\nIn v9, TanStack Table uses the `features` option (via `tableFeatures()`) to declare which features your table uses. This enables tree-shaking: you only bundle the code for the features you need. You can add custom features to the table instance in exactly the same way as the built-in features.\n\n> In v9, features are opt-in. Use `tableFeatures({ ... })` to declare which features your table uses, including custom features.\n\n## How TanStack Table Features Work\n\nTanStack Table's source code is arguably somewhat simple (at least we think so). All code for each feature is split up into its own object/file with instantiation methods to create initial state, default table and column options, and API methods that can be added to the `table`, `header`, `column`, `row`, and `cell` instances.\n\nAll of the functionality of a feature object can be described with the `TableFeature` type that is exported from TanStack Table. This type is a TypeScript interface that describes the shape of a feature object needed to create a feature.\n\n```ts\nexport interface TableFeature {\n  assignCellPrototype?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  assignColumnPrototype?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  assignHeaderPrototype?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  assignRowPrototype?: <TFeatures extends TableFeatures, TData extends RowData>(\n    prototype: Record<string, any>,\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  constructTableAPIs?: <TFeatures extends TableFeatures, TData extends RowData>(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  initTableInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  getDefaultColumnDef?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >() => ColumnDefBase_All<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\n  getDefaultTableOptions?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => Partial<TableOptions_All<TFeatures, TData>>\n  getInitialState?: (initialState: Partial<TableState_All>) => TableState_All\n  initCellInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >(\n    cell: Cell<TFeatures, TData, TValue>,\n  ) => void\n  initColumnInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >(\n    column: Column<TFeatures, TData, TValue>,\n  ) => void\n  initHeaderGroupInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    headerGroup: HeaderGroup<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  initHeaderInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n    TValue extends CellData = CellData,\n  >(\n    header: Header<TFeatures, TData, TValue>,\n  ) => void\n  initRowInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    row: Row<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n  resetTableInstanceData?: <\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  >(\n    table: Table_Internal<TFeatures, TData>,\n  ) => void\n}\n```\n\nThis might be a bit confusing, so let's break down what each of these methods does:\n\n### Default Options and Initial State\n\n<br />\n\n#### getDefaultTableOptions\n\nThe `getDefaultTableOptions` method in a table feature is responsible for setting the default table options for that feature. For example, in the [Column Resizing](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/column-resizing/columnResizingFeature.ts) feature, the `getDefaultTableOptions` method sets the default `columnResizeMode` option with a default value of `\"onEnd\"`.\n\n<br />\n\n#### getDefaultColumnDef\n\nThe `getDefaultColumnDef` method in a table feature is responsible for setting the default column options for that feature. For example, in the [Sorting](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-sorting/rowSortingFeature.ts) feature, the `getDefaultColumnDef` method sets the default `sortUndefined` column option with a default value of `1`.\n\n<br />\n\n#### getInitialState\n\nThe `getInitialState` method in a table feature is responsible for setting the default state for that feature. For example, in the [Pagination](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-pagination/rowPaginationFeature.ts) feature, the `getInitialState` method sets the default `pageSize` state with a value of `10` and the default `pageIndex` state with a value of `0`.\n\n### API Creators\n\n<br />\n\n#### initTableInstanceData and resetTableInstanceData\n\nUse `initTableInstanceData` for mutable, non-reactive data that belongs to one table instance, such as an interaction anchor or an imperative cache. It runs once after table options, state atoms, and the store have been created. Features are processed in a single pass in registration order; each feature's initialization hook runs just before that feature's `constructTableAPIs` hook, so hooks may rely on data and APIs from features registered earlier.\n\nUse `resetTableInstanceData` to clear that transient data when `table.reset()` runs. Reset hooks run after internally owned table state atoms have been restored to `table.initialState`. They do not reset table state slices or externally controlled state, and `table.reset()` does not rerun `initTableInstanceData`.\n\nKeep API assignment in `constructTableAPIs`; initialization and reset hooks are for data owned by the feature.\n\n<br />\n\n#### constructTableAPIs\n\nThe `constructTableAPIs` method in a table feature is exclusively responsible for adding methods to the `table` instance. It runs after all feature-owned table instance data has been initialized. For example, in the [Row Selection](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-selection/rowSelectionFeature.ts) feature, the `constructTableAPIs` method adds many table instance API methods such as `toggleAllRowsSelected`, `getIsAllRowsSelected`, `getIsSomeRowsSelected`, etc. So then, when you call `table.toggleAllRowsSelected()`, you are calling a method that was added to the table instance by the `rowSelectionFeature` feature.\n\n<br />\n\n#### assignHeaderPrototype and initHeaderInstanceData\n\nThe `assignHeaderPrototype` method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared `header` prototype. For example, the [Column Sizing](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/column-sizing/columnSizingFeature.ts) feature adds header instance API methods such as `getStart`. So then, when you call `header.getStart()`, you are calling a method that was added by the column sizing feature. The `initHeaderInstanceData` method is available for per-header instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. It runs during header construction, before sub-headers are populated and before the header is linked to its header group. Headers are reconstructed whenever header groups recompute, so it reruns on every rebuild.\n\n<br />\n\n#### initHeaderGroupInstanceData\n\nThe `initHeaderGroupInstanceData` method is available for per-header-group instance data. Header groups have no shared prototype, so this is their only per-instance extension point. It runs after a header group's `depth`, `id`, and fully populated `headers` array have been assigned, and reruns whenever header groups are rebuilt.\n\n<br />\n\n#### assignColumnPrototype and initColumnInstanceData\n\nThe `assignColumnPrototype` method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared `column` prototype. For example, the [Sorting](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-sorting/rowSortingFeature.ts) feature adds column instance API methods such as `getNextSortingOrder`, `toggleSorting`, etc. So then, when you call `column.toggleSorting()`, you are calling a method that was added by the row sorting feature. The `initColumnInstanceData` method is available for per-column instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. For example, the [Aggregation](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-aggregation/rowAggregationFeature.ts) feature uses it to set up a per-column aggregation cache.\n\n<br />\n\n#### assignRowPrototype and initRowInstanceData\n\nThe `assignRowPrototype` method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared `row` prototype. The `initRowInstanceData` method is available for per-row instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. For example, the [Row Selection](https://github.com/TanStack/table/blob/main/packages/table-core/src/features/row-selection/rowSelectionFeature.ts) feature adds row instance API methods such as `toggleSelected` and `getIsSelected`.\n\n<br />\n\n#### assignCellPrototype and initCellInstanceData\n\nThe `assignCellPrototype` method in a table feature is responsible for adding methods to the shared `cell` prototype. For example, Column Grouping adds `getIsGrouped` and `getIsPlaceholder`, while Aggregation adds `getIsAggregated`. The `initCellInstanceData` method is available for per-cell instance data or caches that cannot live on the shared prototype. Cells are constructed lazily on first access per row/column pair and cached, so it runs once per cell instance.\n\n## Adding a Custom Feature\n\nLet's walk through making a custom table feature for a hypothetical use case. Let's say we want to add a feature to the table instance that allows the user to change the \"density\" (padding of cells) of the table.\n\nThere is no Solid custom-plugin example in the repository yet (the example currently exists for React, Preact, and Angular), but the feature object itself is framework-agnostic, so the steps below translate directly. Here's an in-depth look at the steps to create a custom feature.\n\n### Step 1: Set up TypeScript Types\n\nAssuming you want the same full type-safety that the built-in features in TanStack Table have, let's set up all of the TypeScript types for our new feature. We'll create types for new table options, state, and table instance API methods.\n\nThese types are following the naming convention used internally within TanStack Table, but you can name them whatever you want. We are not adding these types to TanStack Table yet, but we'll do that in the next step.\n\n```ts\n// define types for our new feature's custom state\nexport type DensityState = 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg'\nexport interface TableState_Density {\n  density: DensityState\n}\n\n// define types for our new feature's table options\nexport interface TableOptions_Density {\n  enableDensity?: boolean\n  onDensityChange?: OnChangeFn<DensityState>\n}\n\n// Define types for our new feature's table APIs\nexport interface Table_Density {\n  setDensity: (updater: Updater<DensityState>) => void\n  toggleDensity: (value?: DensityState) => void\n}\n```\n\n### Step 2: Add the Feature to TanStack Table's Feature Maps\n\nTanStack Table uses the keys passed to `tableFeatures({ ... })` to infer which feature state, options, and APIs exist on a table. To make a custom feature key type-safe, add it to the exported `Plugins`, `TableState_FeatureMap`, `TableOptions_FeatureMap`, and `Table_FeatureMap` interfaces with declaration merging.\n\n```ts\ndeclare module '@tanstack/solid-table' {\n  interface Plugins {\n    densityPlugin: TableFeature\n  }\n\n  interface TableState_FeatureMap {\n    densityPlugin: TableState_Density\n  }\n\n  interface TableOptions_FeatureMap<\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  > {\n    densityPlugin: TableOptions_Density\n  }\n\n  interface Table_FeatureMap<\n    TFeatures extends TableFeatures,\n    TData extends RowData,\n  > {\n    densityPlugin: Table_Density\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOnce the feature is registered this way, TypeScript can infer the feature's state, options, and APIs only on tables whose `features` include `densityPlugin`.\n\n### Step 3: Create the Feature Object\n\nWith all of that TypeScript setup out of the way, we can now create the feature object for our new feature. This is where we define all of the methods that will be added to the table instance.\n\nUse the `TableFeature` type to ensure that you are creating the feature object correctly. If the TypeScript types are set up correctly, you should have no TypeScript errors when you create the feature object with the new state, options, and instance APIs.\n\n```ts\nexport const densityPlugin: TableFeature = {\n  // define the new feature's initial state\n  getInitialState: (initialState) => {\n    return {\n      density: 'md',\n      ...initialState, // must come last\n    }\n  },\n\n  // define the new feature's default options\n  getDefaultTableOptions: (table) => {\n    return {\n      enableDensity: true,\n      onDensityChange: makeStateUpdater('density', table),\n    }\n  },\n  // if you need to add a default column definition...\n  // getDefaultColumnDef: () => {},\n\n  // define the new feature's table instance methods\n  constructTableAPIs: (table) => {\n    assignTableAPIs('densityPlugin', table, {\n      table_setDensity: {\n        fn: (updater: Updater<DensityState>) => {\n          const safeUpdater: Updater<DensityState> = (old) => {\n            const newState = functionalUpdate(updater, old)\n            return newState\n          }\n          return table.options.onDensityChange?.(safeUpdater)\n        },\n      },\n      table_toggleDensity: {\n        fn: (value?: DensityState) => {\n          const safeUpdater: Updater<DensityState> = (old) => {\n            if (value) return value\n            return old === 'lg' ? 'md' : old === 'md' ? 'sm' : 'lg'\n          }\n          return table.options.onDensityChange?.(safeUpdater)\n        },\n      },\n    })\n  },\n\n  // if you need to add row instance APIs...\n  // assignRowPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initRowInstanceData: (row) => {},\n  // if you need to add cell instance APIs...\n  // assignCellPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initCellInstanceData: (cell) => {},\n  // if you need to add column instance APIs...\n  // assignColumnPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initColumnInstanceData: (column) => {},\n  // if you need to add header instance APIs...\n  // assignHeaderPrototype: (prototype, table) => {},\n  // initHeaderInstanceData: (header) => {},\n  // if you need to add header group instance data...\n  // initHeaderGroupInstanceData: (headerGroup) => {},\n}\n```\n\n### Step 4: Add the Feature to the Table\n\nNow that we have our feature object, we can add it to the table instance by including it in the `tableFeatures()` call and passing the result to the `features` option when we create the table instance.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({ densityPlugin })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //..\n})\n```\n\n### Step 5: Use the Feature in Your Application\n\nNow that the feature is added to the table instance, you can use the new instance APIs, options, and state in your application. Here the `density` state is controlled with a Solid signal and the new `onDensityChange` option:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({ densityPlugin })\n\nconst [density, setDensity] = createSignal<DensityState>('md')\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    get density() {\n      return density() // passing the density state to the table, TS is still happy :)\n    },\n  },\n  onDensityChange: setDensity,\n})\n//...\nreturn (\n  <td\n    style={{\n      //using our new feature in the code\n      padding: density() === 'sm' ? '4px' : density() === 'md' ? '8px' : '16px',\n      transition: 'padding 0.2s',\n    }}\n  >\n    <table.FlexRender cell={cell} />\n  </td>\n)\n```\n\n### Do We Have to Do It This Way?\n\nThis is just a new way to integrate custom code alongside the built-in features in TanStack Table. In our example up above, we could have just as easily stored the `density` state in a `createSignal`, defined our own `toggleDensity` handler wherever, and just used it in our code separately from the table instance. Building table features alongside TanStack Table instead of deeply integrating them into the table instance is still a perfectly valid way to build custom features. Depending on your use case, this may or may not be the cleanest way to extend TanStack Table with custom features.\n"}
